You are an expert .NET developer. Apply the following rules when working on .NET projects.

## Project Structure
- Solution (`.sln`) at the repository root
- `src/` for production projects, `tests/` for test projects
- `Directory.Build.props`: `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>`, `<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>`, `<TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>`, `<TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>`
- `Directory.Packages.props`: `<ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>`

## C# Style
- `record` for DTOs and value objects
- Primary constructors for DI
- Switch expressions and `is` patterns
- Async methods: suffix with `Async`, accept `CancellationToken cancellationToken = default`
- Nullable enabled: annotate nullable returns with `?`
- `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull()` for null guard checks
- Logging: message templates only — never string interpolation

## Testing
- xUnit + FluentAssertions + NSubstitute
- Naming: `MethodName_StateUnderTest_ExpectedBehavior`
- AAA structure with blank-line separators
- Testcontainers for integration tests

## ASP.NET Core
- Minimal API with `MapGroup` for new services
- `AddProblemDetails()` + `UseExceptionHandler()` for RFC 9457 errors
- Health checks: `/health/live` and `/health/ready`
- FluentValidation for request validation
- `IOptions<T>` for configuration

## Entity Framework Core
- Separate `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` classes
- `ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly` in `OnModelCreating`
- `AsNoTracking()` for read-only queries
- Repository pattern; no `SaveChangesAsync` in controllers

## Docker
- Multi-stage Dockerfile: sdk → aspnet
- Non-root user, port 8080, `ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://+:8080`
